<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alpheios</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Alfeios, Alpheus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Alpheios is the longest river of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, flowing about 110 km from the mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcadia">Arcadia</a> westwards through Elis, past ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympia">Olympia</a>, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-sea">Ionian Sea</a>. In Greek myth it was a river god who pursued the nymph Arethusa. Its upper valley was guarded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> by the Frankish castle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karytaina">Karytaina</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alpheios.jpg" alt="Alpheios" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: EurovisionGR · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alpheios_-_Pyrris_Arkadias.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
